Z||000004||Fiero_Freak||11-27-2000||11:48 PM||||New sensor and all and I'm only getting 15 mpg... I'm just afraid to check the compression.  Maybe I can avoid the rebuild by shear will.  Whadaya think?||||reg||1
Z||000005||PlasticWrapd||11-29-2000||05:41 AM||||Before considering anything else, do the regular maintenence stuff. Plugs, wires, static timing, tire pressure, etc.  Check for leaking injectors and especially vacuum leaks.  Next check the brakes... YES the brakes!!  A sticking caliper will cause a constant drag and make it seem like the engine is losing power.  I had this problem as well.  Both front calipers were not releasing all the way.  One day I noticed a smell like a burning clutch.  Sure enough, the pads were glazed.  I checked the brakes by jacking up the car and spinning the front tires.  The left was LOCKED, and the right would go around about once then stop. At the time, mileage was about 23 mpg.  After replacing both calipers, mileage went back to 30-31.  Calipers are aluminum with bakelite pistons.  Bad combination that likes to sieze up, since bakelite doesn't like brake fluid!||||reg||1
